TIMELINE T=430:26:46.00 KIND=SURFACE NAME="Passive seismic experiment turned off" SRC="SP p. 110"
*
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
* SIMULATION (src/sim.f): the START state, the SCORE (burns) and the
* REFERENCE rows the engine checks against or, with state vector updates
* on, resets to.  The period counterpart of these cards is the
* Spacecraft Operational Trajectory and its per-burn "target loads" (MSC
* IN 69-FM-96, Apollo 10, Vol. I; see docs/simulation.md); these cards
* hold the as-flown values instead.  Cards are KEY=VALUE with a SRC;
* tools/gen_data.py ignores unknown keys with a warning, so a BURN can
* later carry TRIGGER= (when it fires) and TARGET= (its cutoff condition).
*
*   START  T= and a state as a CONIC leg's (BODY=EARTH), END= the time
*          the run stops.
*   BURN   T= mid-burn g.e.t. (the ignition time plus half the firing
*          time, so one impulse stands for the burn: ours), DV= ft/s,
*          BODY= the body whose frame the direction is in, and the
*          direction P= (along the velocity) R= (in the orbit plane,
*          away from the body) N= (along the orbit normal).
*   REF    T= and a sourced state.  BODY=EARTH: as a CONIC leg's.
*          BODY=MOON: selenographic LAT= LON=, ALT=, V=, FPA=; the
*          horizontal direction is taken from the lunar leg's plane at
*          that time (MR Table 7-II's lunar headings are not in a frame
*          we can use; see the a11 profile notes).
*
* Burns left out, all below 2.5 ft/s: CSM/S-IVB separation 0.7 ft/s at
* 3:17:04.6 (MR Table 7-III p. 7-10; the CSM docked back and flew with
* the S-IVB stack until ejection), CSM/LM separation 1.4 ft/s at
* 100:39:52.9 and final separation 2.2 ft/s at 130:30:01.0 (MR Table
* 7-V p. 7-11), whose directions we have no source for.
*
START T=2:50:13.2 END=195:10:00 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=9.98 LON=-164.84 ALT=180.6 V=35546 FPA=7.37 HDG=60.07 SRC="MR Table 7-II p. 7-9, translunar injection"
*
BURN T=4:40:03.25 DV=19.7 BODY=EARTH P=-0.798 R=0.600 N=0.052 SRC="MR Table 7-III p. 7-10: spacecraft/S-IVB separation, SPS, ignition 4:40:01.8, 2.9 s, 19.7 ft/s. Direction derived by us from the Table 7-II ignition and cutoff rows"
BURN T=26:45:00.25 DV=20.9 BODY=EARTH P=-0.711 R=0.703 N=-0.009 SRC="MR Table 7-III p. 7-10: first midcourse correction, SPS, ignition 26:44:58.7, 3.1 s, 20.9 ft/s. Direction derived by us from the Table 7-II ignition and cutoff rows"
BURN T=75:52:49.15 DV=2917.5 BODY=MOON P=-1 R=0 N=0 SRC="MR Table 7-V p. 7-11: lunar orbit insertion, SPS, ignition 75:49:50.4, 357.5 s, 2917.5 ft/s. Direction against the velocity: our assumption"
BURN T=80:11:45.2 DV=158.8 BODY=MOON P=-1 R=0 N=0 SRC="MR Table 7-V p. 7-11: lunar orbit circularization, SPS, ignition 80:11:36.8, 16.8 s, 158.8 ft/s. Direction against the velocity: our assumption"
BURN T=135:25:18.0 DV=3279.0 BODY=MOON P=1 R=0 N=0 SRC="MR Table 7-VI p. 7-11: transearth injection, SPS, ignition 135:23:42.3, 151.4 s, 3279.0 ft/s. Direction along the velocity: our assumption"
BURN T=150:30:03.0 DV=4.8 BODY=EARTH P=-0.198 R=0.980 N=0.000 SRC="MR Table 7-VI p. 7-11: second midcourse correction, SM RCS, ignition 150:29:57.4, 11.2 s, 4.8 ft/s. Direction derived by us from the Table 7-II ignition and cutoff rows"
*
* Reference rows: MR Table 7-II p. 7-9 (and 7-VII p. 7-12 for entry).
* Not used: the command module/S-IVB separation row (3:17:04.6) sits
* about 0.28 deg of longitude (60 km) off the conic through the TLI row,
* while the 4:40 row agrees with that conic to 0.04 deg; correcting to
* it made the 4:40 error worse (184 km against 50 km uncorrected).
* REF T=3:17:04.6 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=31.16 LON=-88.76 ALT=4110.9 V=24456.8 FPA=46.24 HDG=95.10 SRC="command module/S-IVB separation"
REF T=4:40:04.7 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=21.16 LON=-68.46 ALT=16627.3 V=14663.0 FPA=64.25 HDG=113.74 SRC="separation manoeuvre cutoff"
REF T=26:45:01.8 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=6.00 LON=-11.17 ALT=109477.2 V=5010.0 FPA=76.88 HDG=120.87 SRC="first midcourse correction cutoff"
REF T=75:55:48.0 BODY=MOON LAT=0.16 LON=167.13 ALT=60.1 V=5479.0 FPA=-0.20 SRC="lunar orbit insertion cutoff"
REF T=80:11:53.5 BODY=MOON LAT=-0.02 LON=169.16 ALT=61.6 V=5338.3 FPA=0.32 SRC="lunar orbit circularization cutoff"
REF T=100:12:00.0 BODY=MOON LAT=1.11 LON=116.21 ALT=62.9 V=5333.8 FPA=0.16 SRC="undocking"
REF T=100:40:01.9 BODY=MOON LAT=1.05 LON=31.41 ALT=62.5 V=5332.2 FPA=-0.16 SRC="separation cutoff"
REF T=128:03:00.0 BODY=MOON LAT=1.18 LON=67.31 ALT=60.6 V=5341.5 FPA=0.16 SRC="docking"
REF T=130:09:31.2 BODY=MOON LAT=1.10 LON=41.85 ALT=61.6 V=5335.9 FPA=0.15 SRC="ascent stage jettison"
REF T=135:23:42.3 BODY=MOON LAT=-0.16 LON=164.02 ALT=52.4 V=5376.0 FPA=-0.03 SRC="transearth injection ignition"
REF T=150:30:07.4 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=-13.16 LON=-37.83 ALT=169080.6 V=4074.0 FPA=-80.41 HDG=129.30 SRC="second midcourse correction cutoff"
REF T=194:49:12.7 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=-35.09 LON=122.54 ALT=1778.3 V=29615.5 FPA=-35.26 HDG=69.27 SRC="command module/service module separation"
REF T=195:03:05.7 BODY=EARTH LATTYPE=GD LAT=-3.19 LON=171.96 ALT=65.8 V=36194.4 FPA=-6.48 HDG=50.18 SRC="MR Table 7-VII p. 7-12, entry interface"
*
* Burn cue (src/lburn.f; ours, a modern addition): the main-engine firings,
* each opened and closed by two TIMELINE rows of this scenario (SP-4029's
* timelines, by exact name; a pair whose names occur more than once takes the
* first ignition and the first cutoff after it), with whose engine it is.
* VEH=CSM|LM|S-IVB, ENG=SPS|DPS (descent)|APS (ascent)|J-2 (the S-IVB's).
* SRC says where the engine comes from.
* RCS firings (the CSM's separation manoeuvres, Apollo 11's second midcourse
* correction, SM RCS by MR Table 7-VI p. 7-11; the LM's rendezvous burns CSI,
* CDH, TPI, TPF, whose engine no source we hold names) and the S-IVB's APS
* slingshot burn are left out: the cue is for the main engines.
*
BURNCUE IGN="S-IVB 1st burn ignition" CUT="S-IVB 1st burn cutoff" VEH=S-IVB ENG=J-2 SRC="the rows name the stage"
BURNCUE IGN="S-IVB 2nd burn ignition (STDV open)" CUT="S-IVB 2nd burn cutoff" VEH=S-IVB ENG=J-2 SRC="the rows name the stage"
BURNCUE IGN="CSM/LM evasive maneuver from S-IVB ignition" CUT="CSM/LM evasive maneuver from S-IVB cutoff" VEH=CSM ENG=SPS SRC="MR Table 7-III p. 7-10, the scenario's BURN card"
BURNCUE IGN="Midcourse correction ignition" CUT="Midcourse correction cutoff" VEH=CSM ENG=SPS SRC="MR Table 7-III p. 7-10, the scenario's BURN card (the first such pair, 26:44:58.64)"
BURNCUE IGN="Lunar orbit insertion ignition" CUT="Lunar orbit insertion cutoff" VEH=CSM ENG=SPS SRC="MR Table 7-V p. 7-11, the scenario's BURN card"
BURNCUE IGN="Lunar orbit circularization ignition" CUT="Lunar orbit circularization cutoff" VEH=CSM ENG=SPS SRC="MR Table 7-V p. 7-11, the scenario's BURN card"
BURNCUE IGN="LM descent orbit insertion ignition (LM SPS)" CUT="LM descent orbit insertion cutoff" VEH=LM ENG=DPS SRC="the row's LM SPS, the descent stage's engine (SP p. 107)"
BURNCUE IGN="LM powered descent engine ignition" CUT="LM powered descent engine cutoff" VEH=LM ENG=DPS SRC="the rows (SP p. 107)"
BURNCUE IGN="LM lunar liftoff ignition (LM APS)" CUT="LM orbit insertion cutoff" VEH=LM ENG=APS SRC="the row's LM APS (SP p. 108)"
BURNCUE IGN="Transearth injection ignition (SPS)" CUT="Transearth injection cutoff" VEH=CSM ENG=SPS SRC="the row's SPS (SP p. 109)"
*
* SITUATIONS (#17; the model is ours: docs/systems-model.md, section 3).  A
* situation is what a viewer picks: one camera recipe (a fixed set of kernel
* camera code) and this scenario's facts for it.  Its ID is the kernel's
* situation number (view_init, hdr(7)) within this reel, 1..N (each reel
* numbers its own, #26 slice 7e), and kept stable; the situation runs on the
* scenario of the deck it sits in.
* Each SITUATION card is followed by its RECIPE and VIEWS cards and,
* optionally, an HDRREF card.  The card reader reads them into the
* situation tables (src/vdksit.f, layout in src/viewsit.inc);
* tools/gen_data.py checks them and writes build/scenes.json and the
* page's copy into the reel's page.json (build/page/<reel id>.json).
* Every code below is ours.
*
*   SITUATION ID= NAME=
*     TITLE=   the page's name for it (scene buttons, captions, the timeline);
*              ours.  CAPTION= the page's caption where it needs more than
*              the title (ours)
*     GET=     default g.e.t.: h:mm:ss (or s); an EVENT kind plus or minus an
*              offset (UNDOCK+120); or ERISE-60, ERFIND's Earthrise (traj.f:
*              the Earth's disc clears the lunar horizon) less 60 s
*     FOV=     default field (deg), or DISC:f, the reference body's disc
*              filling the fraction f of the frame, rounded to 0.1 deg
*     LOOK=    default yaw,pitch,roll (deg; default 0,0,0)
*     WINDOW=  CSM|LM: hdr(8), 1 a CSM window, 2 the LM front window, whose
*              sill hides what lies below it in the situation's own view
*     LAYERS=  the layers in draw order (vlayer.f): FRAME STARS SUN MOON EARTH
*              VEHICLES COAS SHADOW LPD BURN
*     POSE=    LMPIRO|S7POSE|S8POSE, the kernel pose routine (vdrive.f) that
*              places this situation's own models
*     DRAW=    NOSHADE (no Earth night shading), MOONDISC (the whole-disc
*              Moon: lmoon6.f's extras, craters of 25 km and up, unlabelled),
*              NOLMMARK (no LM marker while landed)
*   RECIPE NAME= and its parameters:
*     LOCALVERT  local-vertical platform about BODY=EARTH|MOON.  MODE=FORWARD:
*              along the motion, down to the horizon, ELEV= deg above it
*              (Earth only); AZ=EARTH turns it in azimuth to the Earth's
*              sightline at the default g.e.t. (AZOFF, after the Earthrise
*              search); TURN=yaw,pitch,roll turns the result (Moon only);
*              OFFLEG=EARTH OFFELEV=: where no LUNAR leg holds the g.e.t.,
*              FORWARD about the Earth, OFFELEV deg above its horizon.
*              MODE=NORMAL: along the orbit normal, local vertical up (Moon).
*     INERTIAL  inertially fixed platform.  ATT=SIGHTLINE BODY=EARTH AT=event
*              FIX= DRIFT= ELOFF=: boresight ELOFF deg ahead of the Earth's
*              centre as seen FIX before the event, up against its drift from
*              then to DRIFT before the event.  ATT=S7ATT: the stack's held
*              attitude (S7ATT), looking down the LM's -X axis.
*     CREWSTN  a crew station: its vehicle's design eye and front window.
*              VEH=LM: the commander's eye on the descending LM's axes
*              (LMDESC), LPDDN down from its +Z, riding the LM.  VEH=CM is
*              the CM station's geometry (vview.f STATCM, in_view 2); no
*              situation's own camera can name it until it has an axes
*              source, so tools/gen_data.py refuses it for now.
*     BODYCTR  above the sub-observer point of BODY=MOON, ALT= km up, looking
*              at its centre; yaw and pitch move the sub-observer point
*     EXTSEED  external view seeded on a vehicle: ATT=S8ATT, the camera DIST=
*              km from it on its far side from the Earth
*     TN D-6853 (printed p. 13) offers "an inertially fixed platform or a
*     local-vertical platform" (as quoted in src/vview.f); the other recipes
*     and all the names are ours.
*   VIEWS
*     VIEW=    WINDOW|EXTERNAL|CM|LM, the view used when in_view is 0
*     TARGET=  the default target of an external view (in_target 0): EARTH
*              MOON SUN CSM LM SIVB; OFFTARGET= the same where OFFLEG applies
*     RIDES=   CSM|LM|NONE, the vehicle the camera rides in its own view
*              (IRIDE); a camera riding the CSM gets the CSM drawn around it
*              in an external view (CSMCAM)
*     CM=, LM= the crew stations offered (in_view 2 and 3; hdr(22)): ALWAYS,
*              PLACED (where that vehicle is placed as a model) or NO
*     FIXED=YES the situation ignores in_view and in_target
*     XSTART=  yaw,pitch: the external view's starting offset (deg)
*   HDRREF   hdr(11-14)'s reference object, where it is not the reference
*            body: OBJ=LM (the placed LM's centre), LMDOCK (S7POSE's docking
*            target, OFFSET= km to the LM's -Y of its tunnel top), CSMTUNNEL
*            (the placed CSM, OFFSET= km along its +X); RADIUS= km
*
* Earthrise: one minute before the Earth's disc clears the lunar horizon on
* the revolution before the landing (ERFIND), turned in azimuth so the Earth
* rises mid-frame.  Field ours.
SITUATION ID=1 NAME="EARTHRISE" TITLE="Earthrise" GET=ERISE-60 FOV=8.0 WINDOW=CSM LAYERS=FRAME,STARS,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES,BURN SRC="the film's first shot (t04.png); time and field ours"
RECIPE NAME=LOCALVERT BODY=MOON MODE=FORWARD AZ=EARTH ELEV=0
VIEWS VIEW=WINDOW TARGET=MOON RIDES=CSM CM=ALWAYS LM=PLACED
*
* Earth approach on the transearth coast: attitude held inertially from 10 h
* before entry interface, so the disc climbs in from below, grows and leaves
* only its limb arc as the spacecraft closes on entry.  Times and field ours.
SITUATION ID=2 NAME="EARTH APPROACH" TITLE="Transearth coast" GET=EI-5:00:00 FOV=60 WINDOW=CSM LAYERS=FRAME,STARS,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES,BURN SRC="the film's second shot (t08.png to t20.png); times and field ours"
RECIPE NAME=INERTIAL ATT=SIGHTLINE BODY=EARTH AT=EI FIX=10:00:00 DRIFT=1:00:00 ELOFF=0
VIEWS VIEW=WINDOW TARGET=EARTH RIDES=CSM CM=ALWAYS LM=PLACED
*
* Earth parking orbit, looking forward 8 deg above the horizon.  No night
* shading: the film shows none from low orbit.  Time, field and angle ours.
SITUATION ID=3 NAME="EARTH LIMB" TITLE="Earth limb" GET=1:30:00 FOV=70 WINDOW=CSM LAYERS=FRAME,STARS,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES,BURN DRAW=NOSHADE SRC="ours"
RECIPE NAME=LOCALVERT BODY=EARTH MODE=FORWARD ELEV=8
VIEWS VIEW=WINDOW TARGET=EARTH RIDES=CSM CM=ALWAYS LM=PLACED
*
* LM rendezvous and inspection, two minutes after undocking: the LM at its
* state turning in place (LMPIRO), seen along the orbit normal.  No stars, as
* on the film (t22.png, t25.png).  The header's reference object is the LM,
* about half its width (ours).
SITUATION ID=4 NAME="LM RENDEZVOUS" TITLE="LM rendezvous" GET=UNDOCK+120 FOV=12 WINDOW=CSM LAYERS=FRAME,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES,BURN POSE=LMPIRO SRC="the film's third shot (t22.png, t25.png); time and field ours"
RECIPE NAME=LOCALVERT BODY=MOON MODE=NORMAL
VIEWS VIEW=WINDOW TARGET=LM RIDES=CSM CM=ALWAYS LM=PLACED
HDRREF OBJ=LM RADIUS=4.5E-3 SRC="ours"
*
* LM descent, the commander's front window in the P64 approach.  The film's
* descent frame is numbered to +-50 at its edges; in the gnomonic plot (pen.f
* PROJ) that is a physical field of 2 ATAN(50 deg in radians) = 82.4 deg.  The
* camera rides the LM: its own view is the LM station.
SITUATION ID=5 NAME="LM DESCENT" TITLE="LM descent" GET=102:42:00 FOV=82.4 WINDOW=LM LAYERS=FRAME,STARS,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES,BURN,SHADOW,LPD SRC="the film's fourth shot (t28.png, t31.png, t35.png); time ours"
RECIPE NAME=CREWSTN VEH=LM SRC="LPDDN fitted to the film's LPD marks (llpd.f)"
VIEWS VIEW=WINDOW TARGET=MOON RIDES=LM CM=NO LM=ALWAYS
*
* Moon view, a modern addition (no 1969 plot type we have a source for): the
* whole disc from 35,000 km above the sub-observer point, which puts the disc
* at 85 percent of the frame (6.4 deg), at touchdown so the terminator falls
* as it did for the landing.  All ours.  hdr(8) stays 1, as before.
SITUATION ID=6 NAME="MOON VIEW" TITLE="Moon view" CAPTION="Moon view — spin to explore" GET=TOUCH FOV=DISC:0.85 WINDOW=CSM LAYERS=FRAME,STARS,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES DRAW=MOONDISC,NOLMMARK SRC="ours, a modern addition"
RECIPE NAME=BODYCTR BODY=MOON ALT=35000
VIEWS VIEW=WINDOW TARGET=MOON RIDES=NONE CM=NO LM=NO FIXED=YES
*
* Transposition and docking, mid-approach, about 56 ft out (S7POSE's closing
* law), looking along the CSM +X axis at the LM's docking target through the
* COAS.  Field ours.  The external view starts 60 deg round and 25 deg up,
* since down the docking axis the CSM's own solids hide the LM (ours).
* Header: the docking target, 0.72 m to the LM's -Y of its tunnel (ours,
* LMDOCK), and half the LM's 14 ft 1 in width (Apollo 11 press kit, printed
* p. 96).
SITUATION ID=7 NAME="TRANSPOSITION AND DOCKING" TITLE="Transposition & docking" GET=APPR+60 FOV=30 WINDOW=CSM LAYERS=FRAME,STARS,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES,BURN,COAS POSE=S7POSE SRC="Mission Report MSC-00171 p. 4-2 and table 7-II p. 7-9 (see S7POSE); moment and field ours"
RECIPE NAME=INERTIAL ATT=S7ATT
VIEWS VIEW=WINDOW TARGET=LM RIDES=CSM CM=ALWAYS LM=PLACED XSTART=60,25
HDRREF OBJ=LMDOCK OFFSET=0.72E-3 RADIUS=2.15E-3 SRC="Apollo 11 press kit, printed p. 96 (the LM's width); the offset ours"
*
* The docked stack in translunar coast, a modern addition (VIEW drew vehicles
* as seen from a vehicle, TN D-6853 p. 12, not from outside both): half an
* hour into passive thermal control (the PTC event), after the LM's
* extraction and before the first midcourse correction, our choice of moment.
* Its own view is external, 60 m from the stack on its far side from the
* Earth.  Header: the CSM's tunnel top and about half the stack's length.
SITUATION ID=8 NAME="DOCKED STACK" TITLE="Translunar stack" GET=PTC+1800 FOV=40 WINDOW=CSM LAYERS=FRAME,STARS,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES,BURN POSE=S8POSE SRC="Apollo 11 Flight Journal 010:58:19 (PTC); moment, distance and field ours"
RECIPE NAME=EXTSEED ATT=S8ATT DIST=0.060
VIEWS VIEW=EXTERNAL TARGET=CSM RIDES=NONE CM=ALWAYS LM=PLACED
HDRREF OBJ=CSMTUNNEL OFFSET=2.743E-3 RADIUS=1.0E-2 SRC="ours"
*
* Launch pad, a modern addition (the view is ours): the External view of the
* stack on LC-39A ten minutes before range zero, the camera west-southwest of
* the pad (yaw -120, pitch 25 deg, as the launch complex shots of #97 use),
* aimed at the S-IVB, at a 110 deg field.  The camera, complex and stack are
* the kernel's (vdrive.f PADPL, ASCPL, LVPL); the recipe is situation 3's,
* which holds the Earth-fixed ascent table's pad state.  Labels (the page's
* own control) are not a card key.
SITUATION ID=9 NAME="LAUNCH PAD" TITLE="Launch pad" CAPTION="Launch pad - Saturn V on LC-39A" GET=-0:10:00 FOV=110 LOOK=-120,25,0 WINDOW=CSM LAYERS=FRAME,STARS,SUN,MOON,EARTH,VEHICLES,BURN DRAW=NOSHADE SRC="ours, a modern addition; the pad and stack from the press kit (see CLAUDE.md, the launch complex, #97)"
RECIPE NAME=LOCALVERT BODY=EARTH MODE=FORWARD ELEV=8
VIEWS VIEW=EXTERNAL TARGET=SIVB RIDES=CSM CM=ALWAYS LM=PLACED
*
* SPANS (#17; ours): how the page puts a situation on this scenario's time.
* tools/gen_data.py writes them into the reel's page.json
* (build/page/<reel id>.json), the page's only copy; the kernel never
* reads them.  A g.e.t. on a SPAN card is h:mm:ss (or s) or an EVENT kind of
* this scenario plus or minus an offset (UNDOCK, TOUCH-600), resolved when
* the tables are generated; END is the end of the scenario.  SIT= names a
* situation of this deck.
*
*   SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT= UNTIL= [VIEW=] [TARGET=] [FOV=] [CAPTION=]
*     The timeline's camera (Review: an event jump, and Following as the
*     g.e.t. runs) while the g.e.t. is before UNTIL; FOLLOW cards in g.e.t.
*     order, the last UNTIL=END.  VIEW= WINDOW|EXTERNAL|CM|LM and TARGET=
*     EARTH|MOON|SUN|CSM|LM|SIVB as on the VIEWS card (in_view, in_target);
*     absent, the situation's own.  FOV= the field (deg); absent, the
*     situation's default.  CAPTION= the caption while the situation shown
*     is the span's, outside Live and the reels (which caption their own);
*     absent, the situation's.  For a situation that spans several phases
*     (Apollo 8's Earthrise situation).
*   SPAN TRACK=LIVE SIT= UNTIL= NAME=
*     Live's mission phase while the g.e.t. is before UNTIL, NAME its
*     caption; LIVE cards in g.e.t. order, the last UNTIL=END.  Live follows
*     the one scenario that has them.
*   SPAN TRACK=JUMP SIT= FROM= LEN= BUTTON=
*     A Live jump button, BUTTON and FROM its text: Live pins the situation
*     from FROM for LEN s, captioned with the situation's title.
*   SPAN TRACK=PIN SIT=
*     A situation that is no mission phase: picked in Live, it is pinned at
*     the current g.e.t. until the viewer scrubs or types a time, captioned
*     with its CAPTION (or title).
*
* Follow.  On the pad, in the ascent and in the entry the external view looks
* at the Earth at a 20 deg field (ours), the disc with its LC-39A mark
* (learth.f DPAD); the CSM is marked where VSTATE puts it, on the TABLE legs
* (traj.f TABRV): standing on the pad, running downrange to S-IVB cutoff,
* falling to the splash point and riding there.  The whole disc shows where
* on the Earth that is, which a camera beside the CSM (TARGET=CSM) would not.
* The spans are ours, from this deck's cards and the kernel's code (src/*.f).
* Burn spans (ignition to cutoff, the TIMELINE rows of SP-4029) follow MSC IN
* 69-FM-197's views of the same burn: its CSM burn plots (figures 5.1-1,
* 6.1-1, 7.1-1) look along the CM's X axis through the left rendezvous window
* at a 100 deg field, which is the CM station (in_view 2, vview.f STATCM;
* CMCAB reads that window off figure 9.0-3).  A span cannot turn the camera:
* those plots have the Earth or Moon at the top, heads down, where the
* station keeps the situation's own up and puts it at the bottom (ours to
* model; a gap).
*
* The pad and the ascent (TABLE leg, SP p. 103) to S-IVB cutoff, where the
* CIRC leg begins (FROM 0:11:39.33).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=3 UNTIL=0:11:39.33 VIEW=EXTERNAL TARGET=EARTH FOV=20
* CIRC leg to the S-IVB's 2nd burn ignition, 2:44:16.2 (TIMELINE row, SP p.
* 105): situation 3's forward horizon view (vdrive.f SCNCAM).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=3 UNTIL=2:44:16.2
* TLI, to the 2nd burn cutoff 2:50:03.03 (SP p. 105): the CM station at 100
* deg, the Earth's limb across the frame and stars, as figure 5.1-1 (PDF pp.
* 53-55, printed 35-37; pre-flight 2:44:11 to 2:50:02).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=3 UNTIL=2:50:03.03 VIEW=CM FOV=100
* The TLI conic: situation 3's forward horizon view loses the Earth near
* 3:00 (our run).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=3 UNTIL=3:00:00
* TLI coast to the CSM's separation from the S-IVB, 3:15:23 (TIMELINE row,
* SP p. 106).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=3 UNTIL=3:15:23 VIEW=EXTERNAL TARGET=EARTH FOV=20
* S7POSE: 100 ft until APPR, closing to the DOCK event (MR Table 7-II p.
* 7-9).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=7 UNTIL=DOCK
* Docked, seen from outside (S7POSE puts the CSM on the LM's axis) to the
* stack's ejection, 4:17:03 (TIMELINE row, SP p. 106); 40 deg ours.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=7 UNTIL=4:17:03 VIEW=EXTERNAL TARGET=LM FOV=40
* Ejected: the CSM's window aimed at the S-IVB, which backs away down the
* stack's axis at the ejection springs' 1 ft/s (traj.f SIVST; Apollo 11
* press kit, printed p. 30), to the evasive manoeuvre's ignition, 4:40:01.72
* (TIMELINE row, SP p. 106); 40 deg ours.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=3 UNTIL=4:40:01.72 TARGET=SIVB FOV=40
* The evasive manoeuvre (the BURN card's 19.7 ft/s, MR Table 7-III p. 7-10)
* opens the range to about 4 km by the S-IVB's slingshot venting (the SLING
* event, SP p. 106), after which it has no state; 10 deg ours.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=3 UNTIL=SLING TARGET=SIVB FOV=10
* The docked stack (S8POSE) on the translunar conics to LOI ignition,
* 75:49:50.37 (TIMELINE row; LUNAR FROM 75:49:50.4).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=8 UNTIL=75:49:50.37
* LOI, to its cutoff 75:55:47.90 (SP p. 106): from the CM station of the
* docked stack the LM fills the middle of a 100 deg field, as figure 6.1-1
* (PDF pp. 113-115, printed 95-97); the figure's Moon limb at the top is not
* in ours (situation 8 holds its passive thermal control roll, S8ATT).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=8 UNTIL=75:55:47.90 VIEW=CM FOV=100
* Lunar orbit (LUNAR legs, LUNORB) to the UNDOCK event.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=UNDOCK
* LMPIRO's LM 300 ft out (traj.f LMSTAT), to the LMSEP event (SP p. 104).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=4 UNTIL=LMSEP
* The CSM's window aimed at the LM on its first leg, to DOI ignition
* 101:36:14.0 (MR Table 7-II); 20 deg ours.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=101:36:14.0 TARGET=LM FOV=20
* DOI, to its cutoff 101:36:44.00 (SP p. 107): from the LM (the LM station,
* LMPIRO's attitude) the Moon below a horizon near the middle at 100 deg, as
* figure 6.2.1-2 (PDF p. 128, printed 110) shows from the docking window;
* ours is the front window, and tilts with the pirouette.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=4 UNTIL=101:36:44.00 VIEW=LM FOV=100
* The descent orbit: CSM and LM marks on the disc, to LMDESC's last 600 s
* before the TOUCH event.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=6 UNTIL=TOUCH-600
* The descent, then the landed LM (LMDESC holds TAU at 0), to the LIFT event
* (SP p. 104).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=5 UNTIL=LIFT
* The powered ascent (no LM state), to LM orbit insertion 124:29:15.7 (MR
* Table 7-II).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=124:29:15.7
* The rendezvous, the CSM's window aimed at the LM, to the LMDOK event (SP p.
* 104); 20 deg ours (MSC IN 69-FM-197's rendezvous views, figs. 6.3.2-1 to
* 6.3.4-1, printed pp. 169-177, are from the LM, 100 deg).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=LMDOK TARGET=LM FOV=20
* Lunar orbit to TEI ignition, 135:23:42.28 (TIMELINE row, SP p. 109).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=135:23:42.28
* TEI, to the transearth leg (CONIC FROM 135:26:13.7; cutoff 135:26:13.69,
* SP p. 109): the CM station at 100 deg, the Moon's limb across the frame,
* as figure 7.1-1 (PDF pp. 203-205, printed 185-187).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=1 UNTIL=135:26:13.7 VIEW=CM FOV=100
* Transearth coast: situation 2's held attitude keeps the Earth in frame to
* about 194:00 (our run).
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=2 UNTIL=194:00:00
* The entry approach in situation 3's horizon view, to the EI event.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=3 UNTIL=EI
* The entry (TABLE leg, MR Table 7-VII p. 7-12) to splashdown, 195:18:35,
* then the splash point.
SPAN TRACK=FOLLOW SIT=3 UNTIL=END VIEW=EXTERNAL TARGET=EARTH FOV=20
*
* Live: coarser than Follow, and the windowed situations (4, 5, 7) are pinned
* through the jumps below instead.  Phases, times and names ours.
SPAN TRACK=LIVE SIT=3 UNTIL=2:50:00 NAME="Earth parking orbit"
SPAN TRACK=LIVE SIT=2 UNTIL=75:50:00 NAME="Translunar coast"
SPAN TRACK=LIVE SIT=1 UNTIL=135:24:00 NAME="Lunar orbit"
SPAN TRACK=LIVE SIT=2 UNTIL=END NAME="Transearth coast"
* Live's jumps: transposition and docking from the APPR event; the
* rendezvous from the UNDOCK event for half an hour; the descent from
* situation 5's default g.e.t.  Lengths ours.
SPAN TRACK=JUMP SIT=7 FROM=APPR LEN=280 BUTTON="Docking"
SPAN TRACK=JUMP SIT=4 FROM=UNDOCK LEN=1800 BUTTON="Undocking"
SPAN TRACK=JUMP SIT=5 FROM=102:42:00 LEN=280 BUTTON="Descent"
* The Moon view is no mission phase.
SPAN TRACK=PIN SIT=6
